  Do NOT read the Brier LEVELS across arms: the fresh arm sits at 0.216 and
  the stale arm at 0.219-0.221 because the batches caught different leagues
  and different game difficulty, not because anyone forecasts better when the
  book is fresh. Every differential above is computed WITHIN an arm on the
  same games, so composition cancels there and only there.

  Does the differential MOVE with freshness? (coefficient on a fresh dummy,
   date-clustered.) Signs: dBrier(exchange - book) is NEGATIVE when the
   exchange is the better forecast, so an exchange edge that shrinks once
   the book is quoted fresh appears as a POSITIVE shift. That positive shift
   is exactly what the staleness caveat predicts; its absence, or a negative
   shift, would mean the handicap was imagined.
  differential                               fresh shift       z        p        n
  (Kalshi - US consensus) when book is fresh    +0.328e-3    1.05    0.292    4,037
  (Kalshi - Pinnacle) when Pinnacle is fresh    +0.408e-3    1.30    0.194    4,600
  (Poly - US consensus) when book is fresh    +0.058e-3    0.14    0.888    4,037
  (Poly - Pinnacle) when Pinnacle is fresh    +0.265e-3    0.79    0.428    4,600

  READ: report the shift at its size and sign. The question this module
  settles is narrower than 'the caveat was wrong' — it is whether the
  equivalence survives at MATCHED freshness, which is the arm where the
  book is not handicapped at all. A caveat that is directionally right but
  small and insignificant should be reported that way, not declared dead.

=== 3. DO THE EXCHANGES SIT ON THE BOOKS THAT JUST UPDATED? ===
  Splitting each game's books at their own median timestamp: the fresher half and
  the staler half disagree by 0.336pt on average (1.1 min apart, n=4,642 games).

  mean |exchange - fresh half| MINUS |exchange - stale half|, in points
  (negative = the exchange sits closer to the books that updated most recently)
      exchange         gap       t    share of the fresh-stale gap
        Kalshi     -0.021p    -3.7                             6%
    Polymarket     -0.030p    -5.0                             9%

  READ: both exchanges sit closer to the books that just updated, which says
  they are priced off current information rather than off a stale field. It
  does NOT say which moved first: an exchange that LEADS and books that catch
  up produce the same cross-section. The effect is also small in level terms.

=== 4. WHAT THIS DOES AND DOES NOT SETTLE ===
  SETTLES: whether the book was handicapped by a stale quote, which is the
  paper's stated timing caveat. The headline can be read inside the arm where
  the benchmark is quoted minutes from start, at full power.

  DOES NOT SETTLE: lead-lag between books. Every price here is one closing
  cross-section per game, so there is no series in which one book could lead
  another. That question needs per-book time series the collector did not
  write until 2026-09-16; see book_panel.py S5 and the limitation in the
  report outline. S3 above is the closest a cross-section gets, and it is a
  statement about proximity, not about precedence.
